# Checkwright's gate-binary target roster — gate-sdk/SPEC.md §Consumer payload.
# One Rust target triple per line; `#`-comments and blank lines are stripped,
# the same line grammar scripts/gates.list uses. This file is the single surface
# asserting which platforms a released gate binary is built for: the publish
# workflow derives its build matrix from it, and the installer reads the payload
# copy of it to tell "this platform was never committed to" (omit and declare)
# from "committed to, artifact missing" (a broken payload).
#
# It ships one target, and the ground is evidence rather than capability. The
# two are easy to conflate here, and conflating them is what makes a roster
# widen on a plan instead of on a run:
#   - x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu is the only platform this repo has green evidence
#     for at all: every gate run, fixture suite, consumer smoke and CI leg is
#     Linux. §Consumer payload's join bound is exactly that evidence, so no
#     other triple qualifies however reachable it looks.
#   - Runner availability is not the constraint. This repository is public, so
#     macOS and arm64-Linux hosted runners are already available to it; what is
#     missing is a run bought on one, not a machine to buy it on. Measured
#     2026-08-25. A header claiming the workflows have no runner for a platform
#     would be reading a fact about this repo as a fact about the world.
#   - Cross-compiling a target from the Linux runner is refused for the reason
#     the bound gives rather than on cost: it would publish an artifact no run
#     has ever executed.
#   - WSL is Linux and resolves to this same target, so docs/install.md
#     §Requirements' Windows-through-WSL claim is served exactly as far as that
#     page already claims and no further. Native Windows is no longer
#     *impossible*: the crate now compiles for x86_64-pc-windows-msvc, held
#     there by the cross-check step in .github/workflows/gates.yml's `gates`
#     job. Compiling is not the join bound. The msvc triple stays off this
#     roster until a run has produced AND EXERCISED its artifact, which is
#     `platform-support-ci-matrix`'s promotion condition, unchanged. An
#     iteration named *unblock* removes a blocker; it does not grant a
#     permission, and a reader taking the one for the other widens this roster
#     on a plan.
#
# This is not a narrowing of the trajectory — the objectives are the direction
# and this roster is the tree. A one-target roster makes the omit-and-declare
# path the normal path for a macOS adopter on day one, which ships that path
# exercised rather than as the dead code the vacuous-green class exists to
# refuse. A target joins only when a green run has produced and exercised its
# artifact, and never beyond what docs/install.md §Requirements already states.
#
# What a second line costs, in full rather than the publish half alone: one
# runner mapping beside the publish matrix, and then the consumer smoke, which
# builds its artifact from the host it runs on and therefore refuses a roster
# naming a platform that host is not — steering or a cross-compiling build,
# neither built ahead of the second target. "One line here plus one runner
# mapping" is the publish half and not the bill.
# gate-sdk/SPEC.md §Consumer payload owns both halves;
# installer/README.md §The consumer smoke owns the smoke's own re-entry. The
# widening trigger is `platform-support-ci-matrix` producing a green run on a
# platform, never the leg merely landing.
x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu
